    <article class="docs-prose">
      <h1 id="stage-14-conditional-text" class="group scroll-mt-20">Stage 14: Conditional text<a href="#stage-14-conditional-text" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h1>
      <p>Publish platform and audience variants from the same topics. Use <code>@platform</code> for keyboard shortcuts and installation instructions, and <code>@audience</code> for content intended for beginners or podcasters. Use <code>@rev</code> to identify revised content for review.</p>
      <p>Create DITAVAL files to include, exclude, or flag content. Add beginner and podcaster maps, then define four more deliverables in <code>project.json</code>: two beginner guides, a Linux podcaster guide, and a review build.</p>
      <p><strong>Time:</strong> about 40 minutes. <strong>You need:</strong> stage 13 complete.</p>
      <p>Recorded diagnostic examples below come from earlier runs. File counts, paths, and stage numbers can differ. Run the gate for your current checkout.</p>
      <h2 id="step-1-mark-what-varies" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 1: Mark what varies<a href="#step-1-mark-what-varies" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><strong><code>topics/installing-audacity.dita</code></strong> A paragraph for one platform, and one row of the choice table per platform.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/installing-audacity.dita\n+++ b/topics/installing-audacity.dita\n@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@\n   \u003ctaskbody>\n     \u003cprereq>\n       \u003cp>You need about 250 MB of free disk space and an account that can install software.\u003c/p>\n+      \u003cp platform=\"linux\">Most distributions package \u003ckeyword keyref=\"product-name\"/>, but the packaged version may be older than \u003ckeyword keyref=\"product-version\"/>; the AppImage from the download page is always current.\u003c/p>\n     \u003c/prereq>\n     \u003ccontext>\n       \u003cp>\u003ckeyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> is available for Windows, macOS and Linux.\n@@ -45,16 +46,16 @@\n             \u003cchoptionhd>Operating system\u003c/choptionhd>\n             \u003cchdeschd>How to install\u003c/chdeschd>\n           \u003c/chhead>\n-          \u003cchrow>\n+          \u003cchrow platform=\"windows\">\n             \u003cchoption>Windows\u003c/choption>\n             \u003cchdesc>Double-click the downloaded \u003cfilepath>.exe\u003c/filepath> file and follow the setup wizard.\n             Accept the default location, \u003cfilepath>C:\\Program Files\\Audacity\u003c/filepath>, unless you have a reason to change it.\u003c/chdesc>\n           \u003c/chrow>\n-          \u003cchrow>\n+          \u003cchrow platform=\"mac\">\n             \u003cchoption>macOS\u003c/choption>\n             \u003cchdesc>Open the downloaded \u003cfilepath>.dmg\u003c/filepath> file and drag \u003ckeyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> to your \u003cfilepath>Applications\u003c/filepath> folder.\u003c/chdesc>\n           \u003c/chrow>\n-          \u003cchrow>\n+          \u003cchrow platform=\"linux\">\n             \u003cchoption>Linux\u003c/choption>\n             \u003cchdesc>Install from your distribution's package manager, for example \u003ccodeph>sudo apt install audacity\u003c/codeph> on Ubuntu or Debian, \u003ccodeph>sudo dnf install audacity\u003c/codeph> on Fedora.\u003c/chdesc>\n           \u003c/chrow>"} lang="diff" title="topics/installing-audacity.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><strong><code>shared/common-steps.dita</code></strong><strong> and </strong><strong><code>shared/common-notes.dita</code></strong> The shortcut that differs by platform becomes two <code>&lt;ph&gt;</code> alternatives. The first carries two values, <code>platform="windows linux"</code>, because the shortcut is the same on both.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/shared/common-steps.dita\n+++ b/shared/common-steps.dita\n@@ -25,7 +25,7 @@\n         \u003ccmd>Select the region of audio you want to work on.\u003c/cmd>\n         \u003cinfo>\n           \u003cp>Click and drag in the waveform to select a region; the selection is highlighted.\n-          To select the whole track, press \u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+A\u003c/uicontrol> (Windows and Linux) or \u003cuicontrol>Cmd+A\u003c/uicontrol> (macOS).\u003c/p>\n+          To select the whole track, press \u003cph platform=\"windows linux\">\u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+A\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/ph>\u003cph platform=\"mac\">\u003cuicontrol>Cmd+A\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/ph>.\u003c/p>\n         \u003c/info>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep id=\"save-project\">"} lang="diff" title="shared/common-steps.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/shared/common-notes.dita\n+++ b/shared/common-notes.dita\n@@ -21,7 +21,7 @@\n   \u003c/prolog>\n   \u003cbody>\n     \u003cnote id=\"backup-warning\" type=\"warning\">This effect changes the audio data.\n-    Save the project first; you can undo with \u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+Z\u003c/uicontrol> (Windows and Linux) or \u003cuicontrol>Cmd+Z\u003c/uicontrol> (macOS).\u003c/note>\n+    Save the project first; you can undo with \u003cph platform=\"windows linux\">\u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+Z\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/ph>\u003cph platform=\"mac\">\u003cuicontrol>Cmd+Z\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/ph>.\u003c/note>\n     \u003cnote id=\"quiet-room-tip\" type=\"tip\">For best results, record in a quiet room and position the microphone 15 to 30 cm from your mouth.\u003c/note>\n     \u003cp>Recommended starting settings for Noise Reduction:\u003c/p>\n     \u003cul id=\"noise-reduction-settings\">"} lang="diff" title="shared/common-notes.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><strong><code>topics/removing-background-noise.dita</code></strong> The same <code>&lt;ph&gt;</code> pattern on a <code>&lt;cmd&gt;</code>, and a <code>&lt;note&gt;</code> for podcasters.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/removing-background-noise.dita\n+++ b/topics/removing-background-noise.dita\n@@ -27,6 +27,8 @@\n       \u003cp>Background noise such as hum from electronics, hiss from a microphone or fan noise degrades a recording.\n       The Noise Reduction effect learns what the noise sounds like from a section that contains nothing else, then removes it everywhere.\u003c/p>\n       \u003cnote conkeyref=\"common-notes/backup-warning\"/>\n+      \u003cnote type=\"tip\" audience=\"podcaster\">For podcast recordings, apply noise reduction before compression and equalization.\n+      Clean the audio first, then shape its dynamics and tone.\u003c/note>\n     \u003c/context>\n     \u003csteps>\n       \u003cstep>\n@@ -46,7 +48,7 @@\n         \u003c/stepresult>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep>\n-        \u003ccmd>Select the whole track with \u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+A\u003c/uicontrol> or \u003cuicontrol>Cmd+A\u003c/uicontrol>.\u003c/cmd>\n+        \u003ccmd>Select the whole track with \u003cph platform=\"windows linux\">\u003cuicontrol>Ctrl+A\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/ph>\u003cph platform=\"mac\">\u003cuicontrol>Cmd+A\u003c/uicontrol>\u003c/ph>.\u003c/cmd>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep>\n         \u003ccmd>Open \u003cuicontrol>Noise Reduction\u003c/uicontrol> again, adjust the settings and click \u003cuicontrol>OK\u003c/uicontrol>.\u003c/cmd>"} lang="diff" title="topics/removing-background-noise.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><strong><code>topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita</code></strong><strong> and </strong><strong><code>topics/exporting-audio.dita</code></strong> Whole sections for one audience, a paragraph per audience inside a <code>&lt;stepxmp&gt;</code>, and <code>rev="3.4"</code> on the step that changed in that release.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n+++ b/topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\n@@ -33,6 +33,8 @@\n     \u003cp>Digital audio is sound that has been converted into a numerical representation.\n     A few key concepts help you make better recordings and edits.\n     If you would rather start recording, go straight to \u003cxref href=\"recording-your-first-track.dita\"/> and come back later.\u003c/p>\n+    \u003cp audience=\"beginner\">You do not need all of this to start recording: the default settings work well.\n+    Come back to this topic when you want to fine-tune a recording.\u003c/p>\n     \u003csection>\n       \u003ctitle>Waveforms\u003c/title>\n       \u003cp>Sound travels through air as a continuous \u003cterm keyref=\"gl-waveform\">waveform\u003c/term> of pressure changes.\n@@ -57,7 +59,7 @@\n         \u003c/svg-container>\n       \u003c/fig>\n     \u003c/section>\n-    \u003csection>\n+    \u003csection audience=\"podcaster\">\n       \u003ctitle>Sample rate\u003c/title>\n       \u003cp>The \u003cterm keyref=\"gl-sample-rate\">sample rate\u003c/term> is the number of samples captured per second, measured in \u003cabbreviated-form keyref=\"gl-hertz\"/>.\n       Common sample rates are:\u003c/p>\n@@ -68,7 +70,7 @@\n       \u003c/sl>\n       \u003cp>A higher sample rate captures more detail but produces larger files.\u003cfn>File size grows in proportion to the sample rate: a 96 kHz file is more than twice the size of the same recording at 44.1 kHz.\u003c/fn>\u003c/p>\n     \u003c/section>\n-    \u003csection>\n+    \u003csection audience=\"podcaster\">\n       \u003ctitle>Bit depth\u003c/title>\n       \u003cp>\u003cterm keyref=\"gl-bit-depth\">Bit depth\u003c/term> determines how precisely each sample's amplitude is recorded.\u003c/p>\n       \u003csimpletable>"} lang="diff" title="topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita" />
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/topics/exporting-audio.dita\n+++ b/topics/exporting-audio.dita\n@@ -48,8 +48,9 @@\n           \u003cp>For MP3, the bit rate controls the trade-off between size and quality.\u003c/p>\n         \u003c/info>\n         \u003cstepxmp>\n-          \u003cp>A spoken-word podcast exports well at \u003cuicontrol>128 kbps\u003c/uicontrol>, \u003cuicontrol>Mono\u003c/uicontrol>.\n+          \u003cp audience=\"podcaster\">A spoken-word podcast exports well at \u003cuicontrol>128 kbps\u003c/uicontrol>, \u003cuicontrol>Mono\u003c/uicontrol>.\n           Music needs \u003cuicontrol>192 kbps\u003c/uicontrol> or more, \u003cuicontrol>Stereo\u003c/uicontrol>.\u003c/p>\n+          \u003cp audience=\"beginner\">If in doubt, keep the defaults: they suit most recordings.\u003c/p>\n         \u003c/stepxmp>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep>\n@@ -59,7 +60,7 @@\n         \u003c/steptroubleshooting>\n       \u003c/step>\n       \u003cstep importance=\"optional\">\n-        \u003ccmd>Fill in the \u003cwintitle>Edit Metadata Tags\u003c/wintitle> dialog and click \u003cuicontrol>OK\u003c/uicontrol>.\u003c/cmd>\n+        \u003ccmd rev=\"3.4\">Fill in the \u003cwintitle>Edit Metadata Tags\u003c/wintitle> dialog and click \u003cuicontrol>OK\u003c/uicontrol>.\u003c/cmd>\n         \u003cinfo>\n           \u003cp>Title, artist and album are shown by most players.\u003c/p>\n         \u003c/info>"} lang="diff" title="topics/exporting-audio.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the attributes</strong>
      <ul>
      <li><code>@platform</code>, <code>@audience</code>, <code>@product</code> and <code>@otherprops</code> are the profiling attributes; <code>@props</code> is their common base. They go on almost any element: here a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>, a <code>&lt;chrow&gt;</code>, a <code>&lt;section&gt;</code>, a <code>&lt;note&gt;</code>, a <code>&lt;ph&gt;</code>. Nothing happens to the text until a DITAVAL says what to do with the value.</li>
      <li>Several values in one attribute, space-separated, mean <em>any of these</em>: <code>platform="windows linux"</code> stays in a Windows build and in a Linux build.</li>
      <li>Two alternatives side by side, <code>&lt;ph platform="windows linux"&gt;</code> then <code>&lt;ph platform="mac"&gt;</code>, are the idiom for text that differs: exactly one survives each filtered build. There is no space between them, because the survivor is followed by the full stop.</li>
      <li><code>@rev</code> is not a filter. It names the revision that changed the element, and a DITAVAL can flag it; nothing is ever excluded by <code>@rev</code>.</li>
      <li>Content without a profiling attribute applies to all audiences and platforms. Add attributes to content that varies.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-2-a-topic-for-one-audience" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 2: A topic for one audience<a href="#step-2-a-topic-for-one-audience" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>topics/podcast-production-workflow.dita</code></strong> Three sections for podcasters and one for beginners, so the same topic reads differently in each guide.
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE concept PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Concept//EN\" \"concept.dtd\">\n\n\u003cconcept id=\"podcast-production-workflow\">\n  \u003ctitle>Podcast production workflow\u003c/title>\n  \u003cshortdesc>A typical order of work for a spoken-word podcast: prepare, record, clean up, level and export.\u003c/shortdesc>\n  \u003cprolog>\n    \u003cauthor type=\"creator\">The Audacity tutorial team\u003c/author>\n    \u003ccritdates>\n      \u003ccreated date=\"2026-02-01\"/>\n    \u003c/critdates>\n    \u003cmetadata>\n      \u003caudience type=\"user\" experiencelevel=\"intermediate\"/>\n      \u003ccategory>podcasting\u003c/category>\n      \u003ckeywords>\n        \u003ckeyword>podcast\u003c/keyword>\n        \u003ckeyword>workflow\u003c/keyword>\n        \u003ckeyword>noise reduction\u003c/keyword>\n        \u003ckeyword>compression\u003c/keyword>\n        \u003cindexterm>podcasting\u003cindexterm>workflow\u003c/indexterm>\u003c/indexterm>\n      \u003c/keywords>\n    \u003c/metadata>\n  \u003c/prolog>\n  \u003cconbody>\n    \u003cp>\u003ckeyword keyref=\"product-name\"/> is a popular choice for podcast production because it is free, cross-platform and capable of professional results.\n    This topic outlines a typical workflow.\u003c/p>\n    \u003csection audience=\"podcaster\">\n      \u003ctitle>Pre-production\u003c/title>\n      \u003col>\n        \u003cli>Test your microphone levels; aim for peaks around -12 \u003cabbreviated-form keyref=\"gl-decibel\"/> to leave headroom.\u003c/li>\n        \u003cli>Record in a quiet room with little echo.\u003c/li>\n        \u003cli>Use a pop filter to soften plosive sounds (p, b, t).\u003c/li>\n        \u003cli>Set the project to 44,100 Hz and 32-bit float for editing flexibility.\u003c/li>\n      \u003c/ol>\n    \u003c/section>\n    \u003csection audience=\"podcaster\">\n      \u003ctitle>Recording\u003c/title>\n      \u003col>\n        \u003cli>Record a few seconds of silence at the start for a noise profile.\u003c/li>\n        \u003cli>Press \u003cuicontrol>R\u003c/uicontrol> to start recording; monitor the meter and stay out of the red.\u003c/li>\n        \u003cli>If you make a mistake, pause briefly and say the sentence again; you will cut the first attempt later.\u003c/li>\n      \u003c/ol>\n    \u003c/section>\n    \u003csection audience=\"podcaster\">\n      \u003ctitle>Post-production\u003c/title>\n      \u003cp>Process the audio in this order:\u003c/p>\n      \u003col>\n        \u003cli>\u003cb>Noise reduction\u003c/b>: remove background noise using the noise profile from the silence.\u003c/li>\n        \u003cli>\u003cb>Editing\u003c/b>: trim mistakes, long pauses and false starts.\u003c/li>\n        \u003cli>\u003cb>\u003cterm keyref=\"gl-normalization\">Normalization\u003c/term>\u003c/b>: bring the peak level to -1.0 dB.\u003c/li>\n        \u003cli>\u003cb>\u003cterm keyref=\"gl-compression\">Compression\u003c/term>\u003c/b>: even out volume differences (threshold -18 dB, ratio 3:1).\u003c/li>\n        \u003cli>\u003cb>Export\u003c/b>: MP3 at 128 kbps mono for distribution.\u003c/li>\n      \u003c/ol>\n    \u003c/section>\n    \u003csection audience=\"beginner\">\n      \u003ctitle>Getting started with podcasting\u003c/title>\n      \u003cp>If you are new to podcasting, start simple: record, trim the beginning and end, and export as MP3.\n      As you gain experience, explore noise reduction, compression and the other effects to polish your sound.\u003c/p>\n    \u003c/section>\n  \u003c/conbody>\n\u003c/concept>"} lang="xml" title="topics/podcast-production-workflow.dita" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><strong><code>audacity-guide.ditamap</code></strong> The full guide gets the topic under a <code>&lt;topichead&gt;</code> marked <code>audience="podcaster"</code>, so a build that excludes podcasters drops the whole branch, heading and all. The topic gets a key, which stage 18 uses.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/audacity-guide.ditamap\n+++ b/audacity-guide.ditamap\n@@ -43,6 +43,10 @@\n       \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/exporting-audio.dita\"/>\n     \u003c/topicgroup>\n     \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/removing-background-noise.dita\" linking=\"targetonly\"/>\n+  \u003c/topichead>\n+\n+  \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Podcast production\" audience=\"podcaster\">\n+    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/podcast-production-workflow.dita\" keys=\"podcast-workflow\"/>\n   \u003c/topichead>\n \n   \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Reference\" id=\"reference\">"} lang="diff" title="audacity-guide.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the map</strong> Profiling attributes on a <code>&lt;topicref&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;topichead&gt;</code> filter the branch: when the value is excluded, the topic is not in the table of contents and its page is not written. A <code>&lt;p audience="beginner"&gt;</code> inside a topic removes one paragraph; a <code>&lt;topichead audience="…"&gt;</code> removes a chapter.
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-3-the-filters" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 3: The filters<a href="#step-3-the-filters" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>filters/mac-beginner.ditaval</code></strong><strong>, </strong><strong><code>filters/windows-beginner.ditaval</code></strong><strong> and </strong><strong><code>filters/linux-podcaster.ditaval</code></strong> One file per deliverable. Each names every value of every attribute the topics use.
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\n\u003cval>\n  \u003cprop att=\"platform\" val=\"windows\" action=\"exclude\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"platform\" val=\"mac\" action=\"include\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"platform\" val=\"linux\" action=\"exclude\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"audience\" val=\"beginner\" action=\"include\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"audience\" val=\"podcaster\" action=\"exclude\"/>\n\u003c/val>"} lang="text" title="filters/mac-beginner.ditaval" />
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\n\u003cval>\n  \u003cprop att=\"platform\" val=\"windows\" action=\"include\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"platform\" val=\"mac\" action=\"exclude\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"platform\" val=\"linux\" action=\"exclude\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"audience\" val=\"beginner\" action=\"include\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"audience\" val=\"podcaster\" action=\"exclude\"/>\n\u003c/val>"} lang="text" title="filters/windows-beginner.ditaval" />
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\n\u003cval>\n  \u003cprop att=\"platform\" val=\"windows\" action=\"exclude\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"platform\" val=\"mac\" action=\"exclude\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"platform\" val=\"linux\" action=\"include\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"audience\" val=\"beginner\" action=\"exclude\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"audience\" val=\"podcaster\" action=\"include\"/>\n\u003c/val>"} lang="text" title="filters/linux-podcaster.ditaval" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>filters/review.ditaval</code></strong> The review build excludes nothing. It flags podcaster content with a background color and bracketed text, and draws a change bar beside anything marked <code>rev="3.4"</code>.
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\n\u003c!-- Review build: nothing is excluded; changed content is flagged instead. -->\n\u003cval>\n  \u003cstyle-conflict background-conflict-color=\"yellow\"/>\n  \u003cprop att=\"audience\" val=\"podcaster\" action=\"flag\" backcolor=\"#e8f4ff\">\n    \u003cstartflag>\n      \u003calt-text>[podcaster]\u003c/alt-text>\n    \u003c/startflag>\n    \u003cendflag>\n      \u003calt-text>[/podcaster]\u003c/alt-text>\n    \u003c/endflag>\n  \u003c/prop>\n  \u003crevprop val=\"3.4\" action=\"flag\" changebar=\"solid\" color=\"#b00020\">\n    \u003cstartflag>\n      \u003calt-text>[new in 3.4]\u003c/alt-text>\n    \u003c/startflag>\n  \u003c/revprop>\n\u003c/val>"} lang="text" title="filters/review.ditaval" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the DITAVAL</strong>
      <ul>
      <li>The root is <code>&lt;val&gt;</code>. Each <code>&lt;prop att="…" val="…" action="…"/&gt;</code> is one rule: for this attribute and this value, <code>include</code>, <code>exclude</code> or <code>flag</code>. A <code>&lt;prop&gt;</code> without <code>val</code> sets the default for an attribute. Anything not covered is included, which is why the three filters spell out <code>exclude</code> for the other platform and the other audience.</li>
      <li><code>action="flag"</code> keeps the content and marks it. <code>backcolor</code> and <code>color</code> style it; <code>&lt;startflag&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;endflag&gt;</code> put text (or an image) before and after it, from an <code>&lt;alt-text&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;revprop val="…" action="flag"&gt;</code> flags elements with that <code>@rev</code>. <code>changebar="solid"</code> asks for a change bar; the HTML5 transform renders the color and the start flag, and the change bar is for PDF.</li>
      <li><code>&lt;style-conflict&gt;</code> says what to do when two flags apply to one element: here, a yellow background.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-4-a-map-per-audience" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 4: A map per audience<a href="#step-4-a-map-per-audience" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>beginner-guide.ditamap</code></strong> Seven topics in three chapters, no glossary, no reltable.
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n\u003cmap>\n  \u003ctitle>Audacity Beginner's Guide\u003c/title>\n  \u003cmapref href=\"keydefs-product.ditamap\"/>\n  \u003cmapref href=\"keydefs-glossary.ditamap\"/>\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"common-notes\" href=\"shared/common-notes.dita\"/>\n  \u003ctopicref href=\"shared/common-steps.dita\" processing-role=\"resource-only\"/>\n\n  \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Introduction\">\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/installing-audacity.dita\" keys=\"install\"/>\n  \u003c/topichead>\n  \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Your first recording\">\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/trimming-audio.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/exporting-audio.dita\"/>\n  \u003c/topichead>\n  \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Quick reference\">\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\" keys=\"formats\"/>\n  \u003c/topichead>\n\u003c/map>"} lang="xml" title="beginner-guide.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Create </strong><strong><code>podcaster-guide.ditamap</code></strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"\u003c?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?>\n\u003c!DOCTYPE map PUBLIC \"-//OASIS//DTD DITA Map//EN\" \"map.dtd\">\n\n\u003cmap>\n  \u003ctitle>Audacity for Podcasters\u003c/title>\n  \u003cmapref href=\"keydefs-product.ditamap\"/>\n  \u003cmapref href=\"keydefs-glossary.ditamap\"/>\n  \u003ckeydef keys=\"common-notes\" href=\"shared/common-notes.dita\"/>\n  \u003ctopicref href=\"shared/common-steps.dita\" processing-role=\"resource-only\"/>\n\n  \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Getting started\">\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/what-is-audacity.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/what-is-digital-audio.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/installing-audacity.dita\" keys=\"install\"/>\n  \u003c/topichead>\n  \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Recording and editing\">\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/preparing-to-record.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/recording-your-first-track.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/trimming-audio.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/removing-background-noise.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/exporting-audio.dita\"/>\n  \u003c/topichead>\n  \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Podcast production\">\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/podcast-production-workflow.dita\"/>\n  \u003c/topichead>\n  \u003ctopichead navtitle=\"Reference\">\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/supported-audio-formats.dita\" keys=\"formats\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/audio-units.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-compression.dita\"/>\n    \u003ctopicref href=\"topics/glossary/g-normalization.dita\"/>\n  \u003c/topichead>\n\u003c/map>"} lang="xml" title="podcaster-guide.ditamap" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the maps</strong>
      <ul>
      <li>A map is a view. The topics are the same files the full guide uses; what differs is which appear, in what order, under which headings. Combined with a filter, one topic set gives as many guides as there are audiences.</li>
      <li>Each map defines every key its topics use. <em>What is Audacity?</em> has a <code>&lt;link keyref="install"/&gt;</code>, so both maps key the install task <code>install</code>; <em>Exporting audio</em> links to <code>formats/choosing</code>, so both key the formats reference <code>formats</code>; the conkeyrefs need <code>common-notes</code>. The product and glossary keys come in by <code>&lt;mapref&gt;</code>, and the steps shared topic is <code>resource-only</code>, as in the full guide. A map that misses one of these fails the build, as the demo below shows.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <h2 id="step-5-deliverables-readme-and-the-gate" class="group scroll-mt-20">Step 5: Deliverables, README and the gate<a href="#step-5-deliverables-readme-and-the-gate" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <Steps>
      <Step>
      <strong>Edit </strong><strong><code>project.json</code></strong> Four deliverables join <code>full</code>. Each names its map, its DITAVAL under <code>profiles.ditavals</code>, and its own output folder. The first hunk only reformats the existing <code>params</code> entry.
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/project.json\n+++ b/project.json\n@@ -10,8 +10,87 @@\n       \"publication\": {\n         \"transtype\": \"html5\",\n         \"params\": [\n-          { \"name\": \"nav-toc\", \"value\": \"full\" }\n+          {\n+            \"name\": \"nav-toc\",\n+            \"value\": \"full\"\n+          }\n         ]\n+      }\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"name\": \"beginner-mac\",\n+      \"context\": {\n+        \"id\": \"beginner-mac\",\n+        \"input\": \"beginner-guide.ditamap\",\n+        \"profiles\": {\n+          \"ditavals\": [\n+            \"filters/mac-beginner.ditaval\"\n+          ]\n+        }\n+      },\n+      \"output\": \"out/beginner-mac\",\n+      \"publication\": {\n+        \"transtype\": \"html5\",\n+        \"params\": [\n+          {\n+            \"name\": \"nav-toc\",\n+            \"value\": \"partial\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"name\": \"beginner-windows\",\n+      \"context\": {\n+        \"id\": \"beginner-windows\",\n+        \"input\": \"beginner-guide.ditamap\",\n+        \"profiles\": {\n+          \"ditavals\": [\n+            \"filters/windows-beginner.ditaval\"\n+          ]\n+        }\n+      },\n+      \"output\": \"out/beginner-windows\",\n+      \"publication\": {\n+        \"transtype\": \"html5\",\n+        \"params\": [\n+          {\n+            \"name\": \"nav-toc\",\n+            \"value\": \"partial\"\n+          }\n+        ]\n+      }\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"name\": \"podcaster-linux\",\n+      \"context\": {\n+        \"id\": \"podcaster-linux\",\n+        \"input\": \"podcaster-guide.ditamap\",\n+        \"profiles\": {\n+          \"ditavals\": [\n+            \"filters/linux-podcaster.ditaval\"\n+          ]\n+        }\n+      },\n+      \"output\": \"out/podcaster-linux\",\n+      \"publication\": {\n+        \"transtype\": \"html5\"\n+      }\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"name\": \"review\",\n+      \"context\": {\n+        \"id\": \"review\",\n+        \"input\": \"audacity-guide.ditamap\",\n+        \"profiles\": {\n+          \"ditavals\": [\n+            \"filters/review.ditaval\"\n+          ]\n+        }\n+      },\n+      \"output\": \"out/review\",\n+      \"publication\": {\n+        \"transtype\": \"html5\"\n       }\n     }\n   ]"} lang="diff" title="project.json" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Change the "You are on" line and the layout</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -2,7 +2,7 @@\n \n Build a guide through 27 stages. Maps begin at stage 02; HTML publication begins at stage 03.\n \n-You are on stage 13: metadata and index.\n+You are on stage 14: conditional text.\n \n ## Stages\n "} lang="diff" title="README.md" />
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Format and check</strong>
      <CodeBlock code={"dogsbay-xml format -i topics/*.dita shared/*.dita *.ditamap filters/*.ditaval\nscripts/check-stage.sh"} lang="bash" />
      <CodeBlock code={"== validate-project  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\n28 file(s): 28 valid, 0 invalid.\n\n== project-health  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\nRoot map: audacity-guide.ditamap (project config); house rules: none (none configured)\nProject is healthy: valid, no broken references, keys, orphans, or broken element ids.\n\n== build  project.json -> /tmp/check-stage-412907 ==\nall deliverables built\n\nSTAGE OK"} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>The build step takes longer now: five deliverables instead of one.</p>
      </Step>
      <Step>
      <strong>Read the output</strong> Under the gate's build directory, <code>out/</code> contains one folder per deliverable. Use the directory printed by your run. Compare these files:
      <ul>
      <li><code>beginner-windows/topics/removing-background-noise.html</code> says <code>Ctrl+A</code> and contains no <code>Cmd+A</code>; the same page under <code>beginner-mac</code> says <code>Cmd+A</code> and contains no <code>Ctrl+A</code>. In <code>full</code>, the unfiltered build, both survive side by side.</li>
      <li><code>beginner-windows/topics/installing-audacity.html</code> has one row in the choice table, the <code>.exe</code> one; <code>beginner-mac</code> has the <code>.dmg</code> row, <code>podcaster-linux</code> the <code>apt install</code> row.</li>
      <li>Neither beginner folder has a <code>podcast-production-workflow.html</code>; the topic is not in the beginner map, and in the full guide its branch is <code>audience="podcaster"</code>. <code>podcaster-linux/topics/</code> has it, without the <em>Getting started with podcasting</em> section.</li>
      <li><code>review/topics/exporting-audio.html</code> shows the optional step as <code>[new in 3.4]Fill in the Edit Metadata Tags dialog…</code> in the flag color, and <code>review/topics/removing-background-noise.html</code> wraps the tip in <code>[podcaster]</code> … <code>[/podcaster]</code> on a pale blue background. Nothing is missing from the review build.</li>
      </ul>
      </Step>
      </Steps>
      <p>Test a missing key definition. In <code>beginner-guide.ditamap</code>, remove <code>keys="install"</code> from the install topicref and run the gate. Validation and health pass, and the build fails:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"== build  project.json -> /tmp/check-stage-413350 ==\nError: file:/home/you/audacity-guide/topics/what-is-audacity.dita:49:29: [DOTX028E]: Link or cross reference must contain a valid @href or @keyref attribute; no link target is specified.\nFAIL: DITA-OT build reported errors (full log: /tmp/check-stage-413350.log)\n\nSTAGE FAILED"} lang="plaintext" />
      <p><code>project-health</code> analyzes the project's default root map, the full guide, where <code>install</code> is defined. Every root map needs every key its topics use; the build of the beginner guide is where a missing one shows.</p>
      <p>Undo the key change, then test the topic structure. Add a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> after the last <code>&lt;section&gt;</code> of the podcast workflow and validation fails:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"== validate-project  /home/you/audacity-guide ==\n/home/you/audacity-guide/topics/podcast-production-workflow.dita:\n  61:13  error: The content of element type \"conbody\" does not match its content model.\n28 file(s): 27 valid, 1 invalid.\nFAIL: validation errors"} lang="plaintext" />
      <p>In a <code>&lt;conbody&gt;</code>, block elements come first and sections last; a paragraph that belongs after a section goes inside it.</p>
      <p>After each error exercise, undo the deliberate change and rerun the gate. Confirm that it prints <code>STAGE OK</code> before continuing.</p>
      <h2 id="what-you-learned" class="group scroll-mt-20">What you learned<a href="#what-you-learned" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1" data-pagefind-ignore>&para;</a></h2>
      <ul>
      <li><code>@platform</code>, <code>@audience</code>, <code>@product</code>, <code>@otherprops</code> and <code>@props</code> on any element; space-separated values; two <code>&lt;ph&gt;</code> alternatives for text that differs; <code>@rev</code> to mark a change without filtering.</li>
      <li>A DITAVAL: <code>&lt;val&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;prop att val action&gt;</code> with <code>include</code>, <code>exclude</code> or <code>flag</code>, <code>&lt;revprop&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;startflag&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;endflag&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;alt-text&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;style-conflict&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li>A profiling attribute on a <code>&lt;topicref&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;topichead&gt;</code> filters a branch of the map.</li>
      <li>One topic set, several maps, one DITAVAL per deliverable in <code>project.json</code>, under <code>profiles.ditavals</code>.</li>
      <li>Every root map defines every key its topics use; <code>project-health</code> checks the default root map, the build checks each map.</li>
      </ul>
